{
  "teamId": 114,
  "year": 2019,
  "narrative": "The 2019 Cleveland Guardians were a team of contradictory impulses — talented enough to win 93 games, yet frustratingly unable to punch their playoff ticket in the rugged American League. They opened the year with a mixed March and a respectable April, Carlos Santana providing early star power with a go-ahead home run in the first week, while the pitching staff absorbed some painful blows late in the month from Ronald Acuna and a Robinson Chirinos three-run shot.\n\nMay proved to be the cruelest month, the team stumbling to a 12-17 record despite flashes of brilliance. Santana's two-run blast on May 4th was the single biggest positive swing of the first half, yet Brad Hand — the closer who would become the season's most complicated figure — was already showing cracks, surrendering a Matt Chapman homer that erased a late lead. The offense was inconsistent, leaning on unlikely heroes like Greg Allen, and the bullpen's fragility was already apparent.\n\nEverything changed when June arrived. The Guardians erupted to a 17-9 mark, ignited by Francisco Lindor's clutch fielder's choice against the Yankees and Jason Kipnis providing timely power. July was even better at 18-6, the best month of the season, with Roberto Pérez and the offense clicking in rhythm. Cleveland looked like a genuine contender, their pitching staff's 3.76 ERA quietly holding opponents at bay even as the bullpen continued to be a source of anxiety.\n\nAugust and September told a bittersweet story. Brad Hand surrendered damaging home runs and late-game hits with alarming frequency, yet the offense — led by Santana's 30th home run and Franmil Reyes' massive three-run blast after the trade deadline — kept fighting. The Guardians finished 93-69, a thoroughly impressive record, but in a year when the AL Wild Card was unkind to them, all that winning amounted to a long October at home, leaving a talented roster to wonder what might have been.",
